# Provenance — charts/README.md § `SOURCE` is required, including when nothing was forked.
#
# ADR-010: "Their charts are a starting point, not a dependency. Where a chart is close, fork it into
# charts/ with the upstream commit recorded in a SOURCE file." Nothing was forked here, and
# `vendored: none` is an answer to "where did this come from" where a missing file is not.

vendored: none
upstream: https://github.com/seaweedfs/seaweedfs-operator

# The upstream API this chart's templates target. ⚠ An API group/version, not a commit: nothing is
# vendored, so there is no upstream tree to pin. The claim is core/v1 and needs no pin.
upstream-api: seaweed.seaweedfs.com/v1

engine: https://github.com/seaweedfs/seaweedfs

# The driver the SeaweedCSIDriver deploys — a third repository, and the one whose source decides most
# of what this chart is.
driver: https://github.com/seaweedfs/seaweedfs-csi-driver

# Reviewed against upstream sources on this date — the operator at the tag the bundle pins, the CSI
# driver at the tag the operator defaults to, and SeaweedFS' own command list at the release
# charts/managed/seaweedfs pins. A tag rather than a SHA is a weaker claim and is labelled as one.
#
# ⚠ WHAT THIS REVIEW ESTABLISHED, because every one of these decided a line in a template or ruled a
# property out:
#
#   * `weed/command/command.go` at seaweedfs 4.41 — the Commands slice is cmdMount, cmdFuse, cmdS3,
#     cmdWebDav, cmdSftp, cmdIam, cmdFiler … and NO NFS COMMAND OF ANY KIND. docs/plan/15's "SeaweedFS
#     FUSE/NFS" is half true: the FUSE half is `weed mount`, the NFS half does not exist in the engine.
#     No `protocol` property is declared; conformance.yaml § owed, `nfs-is-not-served`.
#
#   * `api/v1/seaweedcsidriver_types.go` at seaweedfs-operator-0.1.38 — `SeaweedCSIDriver` is
#     NAMESPACED; exactly one of `seaweedRef {name, namespace}` or `filerAddress`; `driverName`
#     immutable, `^[a-z0-9]([-a-z0-9.]*[a-z0-9])?$`, max 63, default `seaweedfs-csi-driver`;
#     `storageClass {name, isDefaultClass, reclaimPolicy (Delete|Retain, default Delete),
#     volumeBindingMode, allowVolumeExpansion (default true), parameters, mountOptions}`;
#     `mountService.enabled` default true. Default images `chrislusf/seaweedfs-csi-driver:v1.4.20` and
#     `chrislusf/seaweedfs-mount:v1.4.20`.
#
#   * `internal/controller/seaweedcsidriver_controller.go` and `_objects.go` — the controller creates a
#     Deployment `{name}-controller`, DaemonSets `{name}-node` and `{name}-mount`, ServiceAccounts, a
#     Role/RoleBinding in the driver's namespace, and CLUSTER-SCOPED ClusterRoles, a `CSIDriver` named
#     `spec.driverName`, and a `StorageClass` named `spec.storageClass.name` or, when empty, the
#     driverName, with `provisioner = driverName`. `storageClass: nil` DELETES the class. A second
#     SeaweedCSIDriver claiming a driverName loses to the older one (`DriverNameConflict`). Finalizer
#     `seaweed.seaweedfs.com/csidriver-protection`; deletion removes the cluster-scoped objects it
#     created and garbage-collects the namespaced ones through owner references. ⚠ ONE DRIVER PER
#     FILER: `--filer=` is a plugin ARGUMENT (`fmt.Sprintf("--filer=%s", filerAddress)`), so a driver
#     serves one account and the driverName must be unique per account per cluster — which is why
#     StorageFileShares.DriverNameOf folds the namespace into it through a digest.
#
#   * `internal/controller/referencegrant.go` — `if from.Namespace == to.Namespace { return true, nil }`:
#     a same-namespace seaweedRef needs no ResourceReferenceGrant. The driver and the Seaweed share the
#     resource group's namespace, so none is rendered.
#
#   * seaweedfs-csi-driver `pkg/driver/driver.go` at v1.4.20 — the driver advertises
#     MULTI_NODE_MULTI_WRITER beside the three single-node modes, and CREATE_DELETE_VOLUME,
#     EXPAND_VOLUME and SINGLE_NODE_MULTI_WRITER. So a ReadWriteMany claim binds and grows online.
#
#   * `pkg/driver/controllerserver.go` — CreateVolume creates `{parentDir}/{name}` in the filer, with
#     `parentDir` defaulting to `/buckets` and the name sanitised for S3 when it lands there. THAT IS
#     THE S3 BUCKET DIRECTORY: a share left at the default is listable and deletable through the
#     account's S3 API as a bucket called `pvc-…`. `parentDir: /fileshares` is rendered as a
#     StorageClass parameter for that reason. `VolumeId = volumePath`, and the capacity is carried in
#     the volume context.
#
#   * `pkg/driver/mounter.go` — `weed mount` is started with `collection: path.Base(filerPath)` and
#     `collectionQuotaMB: initialCollectionQuotaMB(volumeContext[capacity])`. So every share is its own
#     SeaweedFS collection with a master-enforced quota equal to the claim's request: the size is a
#     REAL ceiling, and a VM's `weed mount` that omits `-collection=` writes outside it, which is why
#     listMountTargets returns the collection by name.
#
#   * `internal/controller/controller_filer_service.go` and `api/v1/seaweed_types.go` — the filer
#     Service is `{name}-filer`, `filer-http` on FilerHTTPPort 8888. StorageFileShares.FilerAddress.
#
# ⚠ WHAT WAS NOT REVIEWED, stated so nobody reads silence as coverage: the operator's `resolveSeaweedFiler`
# helper's exact host:port, whose file the review did not locate — the driver takes `seaweedRef` and
# resolves it itself, so this chart never renders the address the helper builds.
reviewed: 2026-09-15

